# Line endings, decided once instead of by whichever editor or script last
# touched a file.
#
# The repository was mixed: eleven files in pyjab/common/ plus jabelement.py were
# CRLF and the other sixty-six were LF.  Nothing enforced either, so an edit made
# with anything that normalises on write silently rewrote every line of a file.
# That happened four times during the relicensing work -- once turning a
# sixteen-line change to jabelement.py into a 4191-line diff -- and each time it
# was caught by reading `git diff --stat`, which is not a thing to rely on.
#
# `text=auto` makes git decide what is text and normalise it to LF *in the
# repository*, whatever the working tree holds.  `eol=lf` then checks it out as
# LF on every platform, so the working tree is the same everywhere and a file
# rewritten with CRLF or LF produces no diff at all.  The accident stops being
# possible rather than being watched for.
#
# LF rather than CRLF on Windows too: this is a Python project, Python reads
# either, and one working tree for every platform is the property that removes
# the whole class of problem.  Nothing here is a Windows resource file that needs
# CRLF to be read correctly.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces.  `text=auto` detects these already, and saying so explicitly
# means a future binary that happens to look like text cannot be mangled.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.whl binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
